module
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Exceptional.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.CubicFourExclusion

/-!
# Full midpoint radii in the small-image cubic point-core branch

Every critical image in this branch has order two and is generated by a
transposition. A radius at a component vertex adjacent to a common edge lies
in that edge's two-core. Its seed generators therefore have images in one
marked point core. A seed with nontrivial image is at the critical distance,
so its image is generated by the unique transposition of that point core.
Thus the entire radius image has order at most two, measured modulo the
literal endpoint two-core across the retained odd kernel.

At a critical four-edge path the bound is sharp at both endpoints. Each
endpoint seed supplements the opposite kernel in the midpoint radius.
Consequently the radius has exactly the critical line as its commutator
with either seed, and both full critical kernels centralize that radius.
For the selected generating configuration the critical line is central at
the midpoint. Normal generation then identifies the selected span's whole
radius commutator and derived subgroup with that same order-two line,
without assuming a small forward Baumann slice.

The whole-core and literal C.1.32 applications retain the original radical,
cubic projection, both full moving covers, and the plane and Sylow image
alternatives. Opposite-core control, terminal exclusion, and both final
reconstructions remain required.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, *Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two*
(1993), (5.12), printed pp.925--927, specialized to the full point-core
branch; Aschbacher--Smith C.1.31--32, printed pp.135--136. The edge-coordinate
transport is the original cubic transport used in `CubicSelectedFour`.
-/

